// WEB-REBUILD-001 STEP 2 -- "browsers render 3D well; the founder wants a
// real courtroom, not a diagram." A real (if modest) Three.js scene,
// chosen over Babylon.js for a smaller footprint given CourtSim's modest
// needs (a room, positioned speaker stations, a fixed camera) -- see
// reports/WEB_RESEARCH_001.md. Camera position is grounded in
// reports/GENRE_RESEARCH_001.md's cited courtroom-layout research: the
// reporter seated in the well, hearing every speaker without turning --
// same sightline rationale the desktop build's 2D panel already used
// (godot/courtsim_probe/main.gd), rebuilt here in three dimensions.
//
// ROCKETBOX-INTO-SCENE-001 -- "throw in some of the actual 2D or 3D people
// and actual screens, not blocks... make it look like a real courtroom."
// The flat colored boxes below are replaced by the same Rocketbox cast used
// on the desktop build, converted to glTF/GLB (courtroom_scene_builder.gd's
// sibling pipeline -- see reports/COURTROOM_SCENE_001.md Step 4) and loaded
// here with GLTFLoader. Room geometry gets wood-toned materials instead of
// flat panels so it reads as a room, matching the desktop build's Step 2.

import * as THREE from 'https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.169.0/build/three.module.js';
import { GLTFLoader } from 'https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.169.0/examples/jsm/loaders/GLTFLoader.js';
// FIELD-TEST-BUILD-002 -- VISUAL-FIDELITY-001. Per
// C:\Users\chris\Documents\CourtNey-Core\reports\research\COURTSIM_VISUAL_FIDELITY_001.md
// sec 3.5/3.6 ("IBL is the highest-value single change in the whole
// Three.js build... there's a cheat code: RoomEnvironment... Fork it,"
// and its own one-day recipe step 3, "Environment map -- before touching a
// single light"): stock (not forked/retinted -- disclosed, bounded scope)
// RoomEnvironment gives every material in the scene a real, roughness-aware
// indirect lighting term via scene.environment, replacing the flat/constant
// ambient term the doc calls "the single most diagram-like thing in the
// scene" -- applied once, globally, at the scene level, not per-object.
// COURTSIM-FIX-A-WEB-SCENE, DEFECT 8 -- the stock RoomEnvironment import
// that used to be on this line is GONE, deliberately. The comment above is
// the honest history: this build shipped the *stock* module, and both the
// research doc ("Fork it") and the recon report ("stock, not forked...
// PARTIAL on web") flagged that as the remaining half of sec 9 #3. The
// environment scene is now built in this file, from each venue's own palette
// and this room's own troffer and window layout -- see
// _applyIndirectEnvironment(). Nothing else consumed the import, so it is
// removed rather than left as a dead CDN fetch.
// FIELD-TEST-BUILD-003 Step 4 -- "both get SSAO." Godot's own SSAO
// (environment.ssao_enabled) requires the Forward+ renderer and this
// project runs gl_compatibility -- switching renderers project-wide was
// assessed as out of scope for this pass (a broad, unverifiable-here
// change; see courtroom_scene_builder.gd's own comment on that decision).
// The web build has no such gate: SSAOPass is a stock three.js example
// module from the SAME package/CDN this file already relies on for
// GLTFLoader/RoomEnvironment (not a new external dependency), so it is
// implemented for real here.
// COURTSIM-EVENTS-030 -- the evidence-backed parenthetical catalogue and the
// matcher that decides which staged event a spoken line calls for. It lives in
// proceedings.js because it is a property of the CONTENT (the wording a reporter
// is expected to produce); this file is responsible only for making the thing
// HAPPEN. One table, two consumers -- the planner that prints the report and the
// live run below -- so a claim about what he will see cannot drift from what
// the room does. No cycle: proceedings.js imports voice_cast.js imports tts.js,
// and none of those imports scene.js (checked: main.js is scene.js's only
// importer in this tree).
import { createEventMatcher } from './proceedings.js';
import { EffectComposer } from 'https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.169.0/examples/jsm/postprocessing/EffectComposer.js';
import { RenderPass } from 'https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.169.0/examples/jsm/postprocessing/RenderPass.js';
import { SSAOPass } from 'https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.169.0/examples/jsm/postprocessing/SSAOPass.js';
import { OutputPass } from 'https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.169.0/examples/jsm/postprocessing/OutputPass.js';

// LIPSYNC-INVISIBLE-001 Step 2 -- same disclosed presentation gain and same
// value as the Godot build's own LIPSYNC_PRESENTATION_GAIN
// (courtroom_scene_builder.gd), applied in applyArkitWeights() below.
// COURTSIM-FIX-AA -- KEPT, BUT DEMOTED TO A FLOOR. See the block below.
const LIPSYNC_PRESENTATION_GAIN = 1.6;

// =========================================================================
// COURTSIM-FIX-AA, DEFECT AA-1 -- WHY THE LIPS HAVE NEVER MOVED.
//
// FOUNDER, VERBATIM: "Their lips aren't moving... Whenever they speak, they
// light up... I don't have any other way of identifying that they're
// speaking besides the fact that they light up."
//
// THE PLUMBING IS NOT BROKEN. I traced the whole path -- ONNX output ->
// C-projection -> bounded solve -> pose_names -> interpolateLipsyncWeights
// -> applyArkitWeights -> morphTargetInfluences -- and every link is intact
// and was independently verified by an earlier pass. What is wrong is the
// AMPLITUDE, and it is arithmetic, not opinion.
//
// THE TWO MEASURED NUMBERS, both out of this repo's own reports, neither
// invented here:
//
//   1. reports/LIPSYNC_INTEGRATION_001.md, the WEB build's own in-app
//      Rule-130 read-back off the mesh, during active speech:
//          t0 = 0.0299     t1 (+1.2 s) = 0.0196
//      The Godot build, whose solver is fully converged scipy, reads
//      0.0311 .. 0.1793 over the same material. So the raw jawOpen signal
//      is somewhere between 2% and 18% OF FULL RANGE, and on the shipped
//      web path it has been measured at the BOTTOM of that band.
//
//   2. WEB-MORPH-COLLAPSE-001's own pixel proof, for a FULL 0 -> 1 jawOpen
//      swing -- i.e. the entire range the morph has to give:
//          wide / establishing shot : 16 changed pixels of a 6400-px region
//          face-appropriate close-up: 24,300 changed pixels of 368,808
//
// MULTIPLY THEM. That is the whole diagnosis:
//
//   applied = raw * 1.6  =  0.0299 * 1.6  =  0.0478  of full range
//   on the wide shot :  0.0478 * 16      =  0.8 PIXELS
//   on the close-up  :  0.0478 * 24,300  =  1,161 px of 368,808  = 0.3%
//
// ARITHMETIC. A mouth that moves 0.8 of a pixel at the distance he watches
// the room from has not failed to be driven -- it has been driven correctly
// and then displayed at roughly one twentieth of a visible amplitude. That
// is why no previous pass found a broken link: there isn't one.
//
// WHY A BIGGER CONSTANT IS THE WRONG INSTRUMENT. The gain needed to put a
// speaking mouth at a natural opening is raw-dependent, and the raw value
// spans 0.0196 .. 0.1793 across the two builds' own measurements -- a 9x
// spread. A single constant that makes the quiet case visible (about 20x)
// turns the loud case into a permanent scream (0.1793 * 20 = 1.0, clamped
// open); a constant that suits the loud case (about 3x) leaves the quiet
// case at 0.06 and invisible, which is where we are now. Any fixed number
// is wrong for one of them, and I cannot tell from here which one his
// machine produces, because the solver was replaced since that measurement
// (COURTSIM-FIX-K, plain PGD -> FISTA) and the numbers above predate it.
//
// SO THE GAIN IS DERIVED AT RUN TIME, FROM THE SIGNAL ITSELF, per seat:
// track the running peak of the mouth-opening poses that seat actually
// produces, and scale so THAT PEAK lands at LIPSYNC_TARGET_PEAK. The room
// self-calibrates to whatever the model emits, on his machine, tonight,
// and keeps doing so if the solver changes again.
//
// THE THREE PROPERTIES THAT MAKE THIS SAFE, stated rather than assumed:
//   - SILENCE STILL CLOSES THE MOUTH. This scales, it never offsets, so
//     raw 0 -> displayed 0 at every gain. No amount of calibration can
//     invent motion the audio does not support -- the same guarantee the
//     fixed gain carried, unchanged.
//   - IT CAN NEVER BE WEAKER THAN TODAY. The gain is floored at
//     LIPSYNC_PRESENTATION_GAIN (1.6), the shipped value, so the worst
//     case is exactly the current behaviour, never a regression.
//   - IT CAN NEVER RUN AWAY. Ceiling of LIPSYNC_GAIN_MAX, and the peak it
//     divides by has a floor (LIPSYNC_RAW_PEAK_FLOOR), so a silent or
//     near-silent track cannot drive the divisor toward zero and amplify
//     solver noise into a chewing motion.
//
// TWO SURFACES (Rule 166): the Godot build carries its own
// LIPSYNC_PRESENTATION_GAIN = 1.6 in courtroom_scene_builder.gd and has
// the SAME defect for the SAME reason. That file belongs to another lane
// and was not touched. THE DESKTOP BUILD'S LIPS WILL STILL NOT MOVE UNTIL
// THAT LANE PORTS THIS. Flagged rather than silently half-fixed.
// =========================================================================

// Where a speaking mouth's own peak should land, as a fraction of the
// morph's full range. Not 1.0: 1.0 is the extreme of the authored shape (a
// yawn), and parking a conversational peak there makes every line read as
// shouting.
// =========================================================================
// COURTSIM-LIPS-009 section 8.1, APPLIED BY COURTSIM-INTEGRATE-011.
//
// That lane measured jawOpen influence 1.0 straight out of the shipped
// binaries -- judge.glb head prim[1] mean chin travel -15.023 mm,
// counsel_a -14.382, witness -13.623 -- which confirms
// LIPSYNC_JAW_MM_PER_UNIT = 15.02 below. Conversational speech is 13-17 mm
// (roughly a third of a 40-50 mm maximum gape).
//
// So 0.55 was 8.26 mm: HALF of natural conversational opening, not an
// exaggeration of it. Raising this moves toward life, not away from it, and
// the previous value was the reason the mouth read as flickering rather
// than talking. 0.90 = 13.5 mm, the bottom of the conversational band, and
// it deliberately leaves headroom under the 1.0 clamp so the loudest frames
// of a line are not all flattened against the ceiling.
//
// MEASURED CONSEQUENCE, stated rather than left to be discovered: this also
// raises LIPSYNC_RAW_PEAK_FLOOR (below) from 0.0229 to 0.0375, because the
// two are one constraint written twice. LIPS-009 measured Counsel (Q)'s raw
// peak at 0.0333, which is UNDER that floor, so that one seat binds on the
// 24x ceiling and lands at 0.0333*24 = 0.80 (12.0 mm) rather than 0.90
// (13.5 mm). That is still 45% more travel than it had, and it is a
// reported residual, not a silent one. Raising LIPSYNC_GAIN_MAX is the
// lever if the founder wants that last 1.5 mm, at the cost of amplifying
// solver noise on a quiet speaker.
// =========================================================================
const LIPSYNC_TARGET_PEAK = 0.90;
// Ceiling on the derived gain. At the web build's own measured raw peak of
// 0.0299 the required gain is 0.55/0.0299 = 18.4, so 24 leaves headroom for
// a quieter speaker without being an open-ended multiplier on noise.
const LIPSYNC_GAIN_MAX = 24.0;
// Floor on the running peak the gain divides by. 0.55/0.0229 = 24.0, i.e.
// this floor is exactly the value at which the ceiling above binds -- the
// two constants are one constraint written twice, deliberately, so neither
// can be edited into disagreeing with the other.
const LIPSYNC_RAW_PEAK_FLOOR = LIPSYNC_TARGET_PEAK / LIPSYNC_GAIN_MAX;
// Per-applied-frame decay of the running peak. applyArkitWeights is driven
// at LIPSYNC_TICK_MS = 33 ms (main.js), so 0.995 is a half-life of
// ln(0.5)/ln(0.995) = 138 frames = 4.6 s: long enough that one loud vowel
// calibrates a whole sentence, short enough that a new speaker with a
// different level is re-calibrated within a line or two. Decays only on
// frames that actually carry a weight, so a silent room does not drift.
const LIPSYNC_PEAK_DECAY = 0.995;
// =========================================================================
// COURTSIM-LIPS-009 section 8.2, FIXED BY COURTSIM-INTEGRATE-011 --
// THE MOUTH FLEW OPEN ON THE FIRST SYLLABLE OF EVERY LINE.
//
// LIPS-009 measured, live: frame 0 of an utterance had raw jawOpen = 0.0274
// and was DISPLAYED at 0.550, and frames 0, 1 and 2 were all pinned at the
// ceiling regardless of what was being said.
//
// THE MECHANISM, which is structural and not a tuning miss: the gain is
// LIPSYNC_TARGET_PEAK / (running peak). A seat starts at peak 0, so on its
// very first frame the running peak IS that frame's raw value -- and
// raw * (TARGET/raw) = TARGET exactly. The first frame of a seat's speaking
// life therefore ALWAYS displays at the full target opening, no matter how
// closed the mouth actually is. A normaliser whose first sample is its own
// maximum cannot do anything else.
//
// TWO FIXES, because there are two ways in:
//   1. WARM-UP. For the first LIPSYNC_GAIN_WARMUP_FRAMES frames in which a
//      seat produces any real opening signal, the gain is blended from the
//      shipped fixed LIPSYNC_PRESENTATION_GAIN to the derived gain. During
//      warm-up the mouth is driven at the OLD, known-safe fixed gain, so a
//      nearly-closed mouth is shown nearly closed. 15 frames at the
//      LIPSYNC_TICK_MS = 33 ms cadence main.js drives is ~0.5 s, which is
//      the ramp LIPS-009 itself suggested.
//   2. A LIFETIME FLOOR under the divisor. LIPSYNC_PEAK_DECAY has a 4.6 s
//      half-life, so a seat that stays quiet through a long stretch of the
//      proceeding decays back toward zero and would re-slam on its next
//      line. The divisor may not fall below this fraction of the peak that
//      seat has EVER produced, which bounds any re-spike to 1/FRAC of the
//      settled gain. 0.60 lets a genuinely quieter delivery still be
//      re-calibrated up by 1.67x, which is the legitimate case this
//      normaliser exists to serve.
// Neither fix can make the mouth move LESS than the shipped fixed gain, and
// neither offsets: raw 0 still maps to displayed 0 at every gain.
// =========================================================================
const LIPSYNC_GAIN_WARMUP_FRAMES = 15;
const LIPSYNC_PEAK_LIFETIME_FRAC = 0.60;
// COURTSIM-SPEED-LIPS-005 -- MEASURED off the shipped mesh this pass, not
// assumed: web/assets/avatars_optimized_L2_keepnormals/judge.glb, mesh[0]
// primitive[1] (the head, 1621 verts, 52 targets), target AK_25_JawOpen.
// Mean Y displacement of the 200 vertices that target moves most, at
// influence 1.0, is -15.023 mm; its single largest vertex delta is 18.48 mm.
// Every other target moves that same region by at most 2.06 mm. This
// constant exists so the console can report jaw motion in millimetres,
// which is a number the founder can check against his own eyes.
const LIPSYNC_JAW_MM_PER_UNIT = 15.02;
// The poses the running peak is measured from, and the only ones the
// derived gain is applied to. Everything else -- brows, cheeks, eyes,
// squints -- passes through at its raw value: those are expression, not
// speech, and scaling them 18x would put a permanent grimace on the face
// of whoever is talking. MEASURED SCOPE, not a guess: these are the
// mouth/jaw opening poses present in lipsync_reduced.json's own pose_names.
// NOTE mouthClose is deliberately ABSENT. It is the one pose in this family
// whose large values mean the mouth is SHUT, so counting it as evidence of
// amplitude would let a closed mouth raise the running peak and then divide
// the open one back down -- the calibration would fight itself.
// =========================================================================
// COURTSIM-SPEED-LIPS-005 -- FIX-AA'S "OPENING POSES" SET WAS WRONG, AND
// THAT IS WHY ITS 11.5x NEVER ARRIVED. MEASURED, NOT REASONED.
//
// I read the actual shipped mesh -- web/assets/avatars_optimized_L2_
// keepnormals/judge.glb, head primitive, 52 ARKit targets -- and computed,
// for every target, how far it moves the JAW REGION (the 200 vertices
// jawOpen itself moves most) at influence 1.0:
//
//     AK_25_JawOpen             -15.023 mm   <- opens the mouth
//     AK_34_MouthLowerDownLeft   -0.322 mm
//     AK_35_MouthLowerDownRight  -0.275 mm
//     AK_27_MouthClose           +2.061 mm   <- CLOSES it
//     mouthFunnel, mouthPucker    < 0.05 mm  <- move the LIPS, not the jaw
//
// ON THIS RIG jawOpen IS THE ONLY POSE THAT OPENS THE MOUTH. It carries
// 15.0 mm of the aperture; the next largest contributor carries 0.32 mm.
// mouthFunnel and mouthPucker -- two of FIX-AA's four "opening poses" --
// do not move the jaw at all. They purse the lips.
//
// That matters because the derived gain is 0.55 / (running peak of this
// SET). Running the real network output through the real solver (the
// shipped test_vectors.json frame 5 -> C-projection -> FISTA, computed
// this pass) gives:
//
//     mouthFunnel 0.2108   mouthPucker 0.1733   jawOpen 0.0454
//
// so the divisor was mouthFunnel's 0.2108, the gain came out 2.61x, and
// jawOpen was displayed at 0.0454 x 2.61 = 0.119 -- not the 0.55 FIX-AA's
// arithmetic assumed. The inequality is structural and frame-independent:
// while the divisor is the MAX over the family, jawOpen's displayed peak is
// 0.55 x (jawOpen_peak / family_peak) and can only reach the target when
// jawOpen happens to be the largest member. It usually is not.
//
// FIXED: the gain is derived from jawOpen's own running peak, because
// jawOpen is what the eye reads as a mouth moving. Same floor (1.6x), same
// ceiling (24x), same decay, same "silence still closes the mouth"
// guarantee -- only the divisor changes.
// =========================================================================
const LIPSYNC_OPENING_POSES = new Set([
  'jawOpen',
]);
const LIPSYNC_GAINED_PREFIXES = ['jaw', 'mouth'];
// =========================================================================
// COURTSIM-SPEED-LIPS-005 -- AND THE SECOND HALF OF THE SAME DEFECT: THE
// GAIN WAS BEING APPLIED TO THE POSE THAT SHUTS THE MOUTH.
//
// FIX-AA excluded mouthClose from the PEAK MEASUREMENT, for exactly the
// right reason, stated in its own comment: "its large values mean the mouth
// is SHUT... the calibration would fight itself." It then applied the gain
// to it anyway, because 'mouthClose' starts with 'mouth' and
// LIPSYNC_GAINED_PREFIXES catches it. So the exclusion was written and not
// implemented.
//
// MEASURED on the real solved frame above, in millimetres of jaw travel
// through the real mesh deltas (net = sum of displayed weight x that pose's
// jaw-region dY, over all 52 poses):
//
//   shipped 1.6x fixed          jawOpen -1.092 mm, mouthClose +1.000 mm
//                               NET OPENING = 0.266 mm
//   FIX-AA derived 2.61x        jawOpen -1.781 mm, mouthClose +1.631 mm
//                               NET OPENING = 0.434 mm   (1.63x, not 11.5x)
//   jawOpen-derived 12.1x,      jawOpen -8.263 mm, mouthClose +2.061 mm
//     mouthClose STILL gained   NET OPENING = 6.724 mm   (mouthClose clamps
//                               to 1.0 -- a mouth welded shut)
//   jawOpen-derived 12.1x,      jawOpen -8.263 mm, mouthClose +0.625 mm
//     mouthClose NOT gained     NET OPENING = 8.159 mm   (30.7x shipped)
//
// mouthClose was cancelling 92% of the jaw's travel at 1.6x and 79% at
// 2.61x. Raising the gain without also un-gaining it makes the cancellation
// WORSE, because mouthClose clamps at 1.0 first. That is the trap this pass
// exists to catch.
//
// mouthClose now passes through at its raw, authored value -- the same
// treatment brows and cheeks already get. HONEST COST: a bilabial /m/ /b/
// /p/ closure reads slightly weaker than the vowels around it. That is a
// presentation trade taken deliberately in exchange for a mouth that
// visibly opens at all, and it is one line to reverse.
// =========================================================================
const LIPSYNC_UNGAINED_POSES = new Set(['mouthClose']);
// COURTSIM-FIX-AA, DEFECT AA-3 -- THE LIP-SYNC TRACK WAS DRIVING THE BLINK.
// lipsync_reduced.json's pose_names[0] is eyeBlinkLeft and [7] is
// eyeBlinkRight, and active_mask is TRUE for both -- so every 33 ms tick
// has been writing a model-derived value into the very two morph targets
// _animateLife's blink owns, at 1.6x, on the same meshes. The two writers
// have been fighting for the eyelids for the whole life of this feature,
// with the winner decided by call order rather than by design. Blink is
// _animateLife's; the lip-sync track does not get to touch it.
const LIPSYNC_SKIP_POSES = new Set(['eyeBlinkLeft', 'eyeBlinkRight']);

// =========================================================================
// COURTSIM-SCENE-019 -- DEFECT 4. NOTHING IN THIS BUILD EVER CLOSED A MOUTH.
//
// FOUNDER: "Their mouths was moving. It's kind of funny, like they open their
// mouth a lot some of the times."
//
// REPORTED BY COURTSIM-LIPS-009 section 8.4 AND AGAIN BY COURTSIM-REVERT-013
// section 7.5, both times left for another lane. MEASURED AGAIN THIS PASS, on
// the founder's own machine at a real 60.6 fps (not a throttled pane), one
// case_01 proceeding, 7,262 samples over 240 s, jawOpen read back OFF THE
// MESH the renderer draws for all six seats every 33 ms:
//
//     seat              turns   displayed jawOpen when the turn ENDED
//     Counsel (Q)         1     0.0899
//     THE COURT           3     0.1267  0.1128  0.1188
//     THE WITNESS         5     0.1128  0.1378  0.1162  0.1049  0.1235
//     Counsel (named)     6     0.1088  0.0963  0.1013  0.0799  0.0948  0.0810
//
//   15 turns. 15 mouths left ajar. Not one returned to rest, and each value
//   is then HELD for the rest of the proceeding -- 0.0799..0.1378 influence
//   is 1.20..2.07 mm of chin travel (LIPSYNC_JAW_MM_PER_UNIT = 15.02), parked
//   open, on every figure that has ever spoken.
//
// THE CAUSE, read off the shipped code and confirmed by the numbers above.
// main.js drives this file at 33 ms from a setInterval, and on the audio
// node's `ended` event it does `clearInterval(intervalId)` -- and that is the
// whole teardown. The last tick before the interval dies wrote whatever the
// weight track held at that instant, which at the end of a line is a small
// non-zero residue, and NOTHING WRITES AFTER IT. There is no code path in
// this build -- not clearActiveSpeaker(), not judgeInterject() (whose own
// header says "NOTHING here touches morphTargetInfluences"), not the stance
// blend -- that returns a viseme to zero. The mouth is not failing to close;
// it is never being asked to.
//
// FIXED HERE AND NOT IN main.js, DELIBERATELY. A mouth at rest is a property
// of the FACE, not of the audio node that happened to stop; making it the
// caller's job is what produced three reports and no fix, and it would break
// again the next time anyone added a second driver (a readback, a barked
// objection, a GLM interjection). This file owns the influences, so this file
// closes them, for every seat, whatever stopped feeding it and why.
//
// THE TWO NUMBERS, chosen against measurements and not taste:
//   MOUTH_REST_IDLE_MS -- how long a seat must go unfed before it is
//       considered finished. main.js's tick is LIPSYNC_TICK_MS = 33 ms, so
//       this must clear a normal tick with room for a dropped frame or two;
//       120 ms is 3.6 ticks. COURTSIM-SPEECH-008 measured the median
//       inter-utterance gap at 126 ms, so a same-seat back-to-back line
//       begins closing at the very end of the gap and is re-opened by the
//       next line -- which is what a real mouth does between sentences.
//   MOUTH_REST_MS -- how long the close takes. A human jaw closes from an
//       open vowel in roughly 150-200 ms; 180 ms at 60 fps is ~11 frames, so
//       it is a movement rather than a snap, and a tick that arrives late
//       mid-word re-opens from a partially closed mouth instead of popping.
const MOUTH_REST_IDLE_MS = 120;
const MOUTH_REST_MS = 180;

// ===========================================================================
// COURTSIM-FIX-A-WEB-SCENE -- DEFECT 1 + DEFECT 2 (recon D-01 / D-02, web).
// THE ROOT CAUSE AND ITS CONSEQUENCE, FIXED TOGETHER.
//
// What was wrong, restated from the numbers actually in this file before
// this pass:
//
//   chair() (below) builds a seat pad whose top face is pos[1] + 0.08 and
//   four legs whose bottom face is pos[1] - 0.37. Its own seat-top-to-leg-
//   bottom span is therefore 0.45 m -- a correctly proportioned chair. But
//   EVERY call site passed pos[1] = 0, so every chair in the room had its
//   legs 0.37 m UNDER the floor plane and its seat surface at y = 0.08.
//   An 8-centimetre chair.
//
//   The seating constants above were then measured AGAINST that 8 cm chair
//   (FIELD-TEST-FINISH-001's own comment, preserved below for the record):
//   each avatar's root was dropped until its live Bip01_Pelvis world Y
//   landed exactly on the furniture surface under it. Landing the pelvis on
//   an 8 cm surface means dropping the root by ~0.84 m, which is why
//   THE CLERK's root literal read -0.843290329 and its seated root read
//   -0.993 -- a metre below the floor, feet underground, legs inside the
//   desk box. The compensation was arithmetically self-consistent and
//   visually catastrophic. The furniture had to come up; the people should
//   never have gone down.
//
// The original measurement is still the most valuable number in the file,
// so it is KEPT and re-used rather than thrown away. Reading it back the
// other way round gives the one quantity that actually matters -- how far
// the pelvis sits above the model's own root -- because thigh/calf rotation
// pivots about the hip joint and therefore never moves the hip itself
// (the same fact the desktop build's DICTATION-001 comment records):
//
//   pelvisRise = measuredPelvisY - (oldLiteral - 0.15)
//     THE COURT        1.395 - (0.5 - 0.15) = 1.045
//     THE WITNESS      1.223 - (0.3 - 0.15) = 1.073
//     THE CLERK        0.923 - (0.0 - 0.15) = 1.073
//     Counsel (Q)      0.895 - (0.0 - 0.15) = 1.045
//     Counsel (named)  0.923 - (0.0 - 0.15) = 1.073
//     jurors 1/3/5     0.895 - (0.0 - 0.15) = 1.045
//     jurors 2/4/6     0.923 - (0.0 - 0.15) = 1.073
//
// Exactly two values, 1.045 and 1.073 -- the two Rocketbox source rigs in
// this cast. 1.045-1.073 m is a real adult standing hip-joint height, which
// is the independent check that this reading of the measurement is right.
// _registerIdleAnimation() below now READS this per avatar off the live
// loaded rig (Bip01_Pelvis world Y minus model.position.y) instead of
// trusting either number, and falls back to 1.045 only if the bone is
// missing -- so the constants above become a cross-check, not an input.
//
// pos[1] therefore no longer means "a hand-tuned sink depth." It now means
// exactly one thing: THE Y OF THE SURFACE THIS FIGURE'S FEET STAND ON.
// A separate seatY names the surface their seat pan is on. _animateStance()
// interpolates the root between the two, so "All rise" raises them onto
// their own feet instead of sinking them further (DEFECT 2, second half --
// the old code used rootRestY, a seated-only constant, as the STANDING rest
// height, which is why _riseAll() buried THE CLERK to the thigh).
// ===========================================================================

// Furniture surface heights. Every one of these is also used directly by
// _buildCourtroomRoom() below, so a seat and the box it sits on can never
// drift apart again -- that drift is what D-01/D-02 actually were.
const FLOOR_Y = 0.0;
// chair(): seat top = pos[1] + 0.08, leg bottom = pos[1] - 0.37.
// Pass a support surface + CHAIR_RISE and the legs land on it exactly.
const CHAIR_RISE = 0.37;          // pos[1] offset that puts the legs on the support surface
const CHAIR_SEAT_H = 0.45;        // resulting seat-pan height above that surface (0.37 + 0.08)
// A seated hip joint is not level with the cushion -- it rides just above
// it on thigh/buttock mass. 0.06 m is an anatomical allowance, NOT a
// measurement from this scene; stated as an assumption so a field test can
// correct it with one number.
// =========================================================================
// COURTSIM-SEATED-047 -- THE FIELD TEST CAME BACK AND THIS NUMBER WAS WRONG
// ON EVERY SEAT IN EVERY VENUE. It is kept, because it is still the right
// STARTING value and the only thing a rig that cannot be measured can fall
// back to, but nothing that can be measured uses it any more: the real lift
// is now _measureSeatedPelvisDrop(), per figure, off that figure's own mesh.
// See that method for the fifteen before-numbers and for why a constant here
// could never have been right -- this cast has two rigs and twelve bodies,
// and the measured lift runs from 0.101 m to 0.179 m.
// =========================================================================
const SEAT_PELVIS_LIFT = 0.06;
// How wide a column around the pelvis bone counts as "the part of you that is
// on the chair". See _lowestInPelvisColumn for why this is a column and not a
// bone-weight set, and why 0.16 and not 0.20.
const SEAT_COLUMN_R = 0.16;
// How far IN FRONT of the hip joint still counts as "on the seat". See
// _lowestInPelvisColumn: past this, what the column finds is a sloping thigh
// or a hanging garment, not a contact. 20 mm is the front of the sit bone.
const SEAT_COLUMN_FWD = 0.02;
// A measured lift outside this band is not believable on a human figure and
// means the mesh, not the seat, is unusual -- a long coat weighted to the
// pelvis would drape below the buttock and float the whole person. Outside
// the band the figure keeps SEAT_PELVIS_LIFT and the receipt says so.
const SEAT_LIFT_MIN = 0.045;
const SEAT_LIFT_MAX = 0.230;
// =========================================================================
// COURTSIM-ROOM-002, item F -- THE ARMREST, IN ONE PLACE.
//
// chair() draws the arm cap at chair-local [-+0.235, 0.26, 0.03] with size
// [0.05, 0.04, 0.34], so its TOP face is 0.28 above the chair group's own
// origin. The seated arm solve has to land a hand on that face; before this
// pass nothing did, and the witness's forearm ran straight through the cap
// (MEASURED: 0.040 m of forearm inside WitnessChairArm, both sides). These
// three constants exist so the mesh and the solve read the same numbers.
//
// CHAIR_ARM_TOP is measured from the chair GROUP origin, not from the seat
// pan, because that is the frame chair() authors in. The pan top is 0.08
// above the same origin, so the cap is 0.20 above the pan.
// =========================================================================
const CHAIR_ARM_DX = 0.235;      // arm cap centre, chair-local X
const CHAIR_ARM_DZ = 0.03;       // arm cap centre, chair-local Z
const CHAIR_ARM_TOP = 0.28;      // arm cap TOP face, above the chair group origin
// =========================================================================
// COURTSIM-WEB-007 -- THE COUNSEL TABLES AND THE REPORTER STATION, IN ONE
// PLACE, BECAUSE THEY ARE ONE GEOMETRY PROBLEM.
//
// FOUNDER, VERBATIM (two separate remarks that only have one joint answer):
//   "the counsel booth that's closer to the jury stand, we could move it a
//    little bit closer to the other counsel table and further away from the
//    jury, because the table is kind of close to the jury stand... then
//    it'll be easier to keep both of them in sight."
//   "the one counsel on my right side as I'm looking, so that's further away
//    from the jury, is now cut off too, even when I'm at that angle."
//   "I want the court reporter desk moved closer to the clerk... the start
//    view is like the person's looking at an angle to the left. So we should
//    put it more closer to the clerk, so that looking angle they're making
//    to the left kind of can include both of them."
//   "just don't make it lopsided almost."
//
// THE TEST IS GEOMETRIC AND IT WAS SOLVED, NOT GUESSED. Every number below
// was measured off the LIVE build in the founder's own Chrome (localhost
// :8080, three.js r169, real WebGL, real GLBs) before anything moved.
//
// MEASURED BEFORE, reporter preset, aspect 1.2752 (the founder's own window
// -- his screenshots measure 1060x830 in the canvas, and camera_controller
// .js's own comment names the same a=1.2752). The applied vertical field is
// 55.000 deg, pinned by NAV_VFOV_CEILING_DEG, so the horizontal half-field
// is atan(tan(27.5) * 1.2752) = 33.577 deg:
//
//   eye (-1.150, 1.200, -2.200)   camera yaw +17.32 deg (NOT the +10.05 the
//                                 old comment claimed -- the aim target
//                                 [0,1.28,1.5] was never the bisector it
//                                 was documented as; measured off the live
//                                 camera quaternion)
//   CounselTableA  horizontal  +35.441 .. +68.649 deg   ENTIRELY OUTSIDE
//                              nearest corner is 1.864 deg past the edge,
//                              far corner 35.072 deg past it
//   CounselTableB  horizontal  -47.788 .. -27.707 deg   HALF OUTSIDE
//                              far corner 14.211 deg past the edge
//
// That is exactly the founder's report, in degrees: the -X table (his
// screen right, the one away from the jury) is gone completely and the
// jury-side one is cut.
//
// WHAT THE ARITHMETIC SAYS CANNOT BE DONE. Both tables inside 2 x 33.577
// deg needs their combined outer span to fit that field. At the shipped
// layout the span from CounselTableB's far corner to CounselTableA's far
// corner is 115.93 deg. Holding the eye still and moving only table B, the
// required span never fits: table B would have to sit at x -1.44, i.e. left
// of the room's centreline. The reporter alone cannot do it either -- every
// metre he moves toward the clerk narrows one table's bearing and widens
// the other's by nearly as much. IT TAKES BOTH LEVERS, which is why the
// brief asked for both, and it takes the tables moving SYMMETRICALLY --
// moving only the jury-side one leaves the pair off-centre in the well,
// which is the "lopsided" he named.
//
// THE SOLVE, from a grid search over (reporter x, reporter z, table
// half-separation, table z) with the table footprints' eight outer corners
// as the test and a 2.0 m minimum centre aisle as the constraint:
//
//   COUNSEL_TABLE_X 3.30 -> 2.10   (each table 1.20 m inboard, symmetric)
//   REPORTER_X     -1.15 -> -3.20  (2.05 m toward the clerk, as asked)
//   table z, table size, everything else: UNCHANGED
//
// PREDICTED AFTER, same eye height, same 55 deg lens, aim re-derived to the
// true bisector: table half-span 32.87 deg against a 33.577 deg half-field,
// i.e. +0.71 deg of margin on the binding corner, and +9.91 deg at 16:9.
// The measured after-numbers are in reports/COURTSIM_WEB_007.md and they
// are what this change is judged on, not this paragraph.
//
// WHAT IT COSTS, STATED RATHER THAN BURIED:
//   * The margin is 0.71 deg. That is thin. It is thin because the room is
//     physically too wide for a 55 deg lens from a seat 3.7 m away, and the
//     lens is locked by another lane. If the founder widens his window the
//     margin grows; if he narrows it past a = 1.2752 the outer corners go
//     back out. The single number to change is COUNSEL_TABLE_X.
//   * The default view now yaws +34.9 deg instead of +17.3. That is a
//     stronger diagonal, and it is the one he asked for in the same breath
//     ("that looking angle they're making to the left ... can include both
//     of them").
//   * Counsel A is now much nearer the eye than counsel B (3.86 m against
//     6.47 m). The pair is symmetric about the WELL, not about the reporter,
//     so one lawyer reads larger than the other. Residual asymmetry, in
//     metres, is reported in full. It is the price of a symmetric room.
//
// The jury-side table's outer edge moves from x 4.33 to x 3.13 -- from
// 0.12 m off the jury rail's outer face (4.45) to 1.32 m off it. That alone
// is the "the table is kind of close to the jury stand" complaint, and it
// is the part of this change with no downside at all.
// =========================================================================
// 2.00, not the 2.10 the flat-bearing grid search first returned. MEASURED
// against the live camera at a = 1.2752: 2.10 put CounselTableA's outer
// corner at +33.601 deg against a 33.577 deg half-field -- 0.024 degrees
// OUTSIDE. The grid search bisects GROUND bearings; the frustum test is in
// CAMERA space, where the 1.18 deg upward pitch and the tables' own 0.75 m
// height pull the top and bottom corners apart by a few hundredths of a
// degree. 0.024 deg is not a real error, but "just outside" is still outside
// and a margin that thin would not survive the founder resizing his window.
// 2.00 buys real daylight; the measured after-margins are in the report.
const COUNSEL_TABLE_X = 2.00;    // |x| of both counsel tables and their chairs
const COUNSEL_SEAT_Z = 1.5;      // where counsel sit, behind their own table
const COUNSEL_TABLE_Z = 0.8;     // table centre z (UNCHANGED; here so it cannot drift)
const COUNSEL_TABLE_W = 2.0;     // table width  (UNCHANGED)
const COUNSEL_TABLE_D = 0.8;     // table depth  (UNCHANGED)
// desk() overhangs its top slab by 0.03 on every side, so the TOP's real
// footprint -- which the seated hand solve has to test against -- is:
const COUNSEL_TOP_X0 = COUNSEL_TABLE_X - COUNSEL_TABLE_W / 2 - 0.03;
const COUNSEL_TOP_X1 = COUNSEL_TABLE_X + COUNSEL_TABLE_W / 2 + 0.03;
const COUNSEL_TOP_Z0 = COUNSEL_TABLE_Z - COUNSEL_TABLE_D / 2 - 0.03;
const COUNSEL_TOP_Z1 = COUNSEL_TABLE_Z + COUNSEL_TABLE_D / 2 + 0.03;

// =========================================================================
// COURTSIM-IDLE-PROPS-012 -- WHAT IS ON THE COUNSEL TABLES, AND WHY EACH
// HAND IS WHERE IT IS.
//
// FOUNDER: "the people on the counsel's table, maybe we should put some
// papers or some laptops on there, or other things that might be on a
// counsel's table, so it's not just a blank table. And it looks like there's
// a REASON their hands are there." And: "Look up all those ways their arms
// and hands could be and see what would fit well in there."
//
// So the vocabulary was researched rather than invented. What trial
// advocacy writing actually says about a seated advocate's hands:
//   * Counsel should "find a comfortable resting place for their arms and
//     hands" while seated -- a flat, settled hand is the baseline, and
//     FIDGETING hands are named as the thing that reads as nervous. That is
//     a direct argument for pinned contact over a wobbling wrist.
//   * The "lowered steeple" (fingertips together, hands low) is documented
//     as the LISTENING posture, distinct from the raised steeple used while
//     speaking -- the right register for counsel who is not on their feet.
//   * Hands touching each other are discouraged for advocates because they
//     read as a barrier, which is why no figure here gets clasped hands at
//     the table.
//   * What is physically on the table is well attested: legal pads (the
//     staple -- counsel and client pass them back and forth during trial),
//     a trial binder, manila folders of exhibits, loose paper, pens, and a
//     water glass.
//
// SO EACH HAND IS GIVEN A REASON, AND THE TWO TABLES DO NOT MIRROR:
//   counsel_a L  flat palm resting ON a legal pad      (pose open_soft)
//   counsel_a R  holding a pen                          (pose pen_hold)
//   counsel_b L  resting ON a closed manila folder      (pose relaxed)
//   counsel_b R  flat palm on the bare tabletop         (pose open_soft)
// Table A is dressed pad-left / papers-and-glass-right; table B is dressed
// folder-left / binder-centre / laptop-right. Different objects, different
// sides, different hand reasons.
//
// `lift` is the prop's own thickness. A hand that rests ON a prop is a
// CONTACT POINT in exactly the same sense as one on the tabletop: its
// support height becomes the prop's top face, the load-time solve drives the
// lowest hand vertex to THAT plane, and _animateContactPins() then holds it
// there for the whole idle cycle. A prop under a hand is not decoration; it
// moves the plane the hand is pinned to.
//
// EVERY FOOTPRINT BELOW WAS CHECKED against the measured tabletop extents
// (table A x -3.03..-0.97, table B x 0.97..3.03, both z 0.37..1.23, top
// 0.750) and against the measured world positions of the four counsel hands
// (a.L x -2.242, a.R x -1.773, both z 1.081; b.L x 1.836, b.R x 2.149, both
// z 1.147). No prop intersects the table, another prop, or a hand it is not
// meant to be under. The clearances are printed on boot by
// _buildCounselTableProps() rather than asserted here.
// =========================================================================
// =========================================================================
// COURTSIM-PROPS-033, ITEM 3 -- THE FOUNDER SPECIFIED EACH HAND. THIS TABLE
// IS HIS INSTRUCTION, TRANSCRIBED.
//
//   "now that I can see counsel table A a little further out, the position
//    he has the pen looks like he's just casually holding it. That's not
//    bad."
//        -> counsel_a R KEEPS the casual pen hold. Not regressed: it gets a
//           real pen and real finger contact and the same shallow at-rest
//           barrel angle he looked at and approved.
//
//   "one of the table A, so I guess the one without the pen, that one can go
//    like by his side more, or how the other people are -- their hands and
//    arms are over the armrest and then cupping the end. That way we have
//    variation, a little bit of everything."
//        -> counsel_a L comes OFF the legal pad and onto a chair arm, like
//           the jury and the witness. That requires counsel chairs to HAVE
//           arms, which in the courtroom they did not -- see SEATS.
//
//   "maybe counsel table B should have she's holding and writing the pen.
//    Like over the paper. Not necessarily doing it, but then like she has
//    the pen like that in her hand."
//        -> counsel_b L takes a WRITING grip over a legal pad. Left, not
//           right, and that is a judgement call stated plainly: her RIGHT
//           hand is the one that reaches the laptop (measured: her right
//           hand is 0.314 m from the machine, her left 0.594 m), and he
//           asked for both. Moving the laptop instead would undo the
//           placement he has already accepted.
//
//   "for counsel table B too... let's make it so the arm is extended like
//    she's typing on the computer too. They moved the computer a little bit,
//    but then her arm is still there. So make it so her hand looks like it's
//    typing. Or it's just on it, her hand is just like on it."
//        -> counsel_b R goes onto the laptop's KEYBOARD DECK. `at` names the
//           world point, because unlike every other hand in this table that
//           one has to land on a specific object rather than wherever the
//           arm happens to reach.
//
//   "But we also want them all to have real looking pens."
//        -> two pens now, and both are built by makePenMesh().
//
// Every one of these four hands rests on something, so every one of them is
// a CONTACT POINT and takes COURTSIM-IDLE-PROPS-012's pinned treatment. The
// laptop hand is the new one and it is pinned exactly like the others.
// =========================================================================
const COUNSEL_HAND_PROPS = {
  counsel_a: {
    // armrest: this hand skips the desk pass entirely and is picked up by the
    // chair-arm solve, the same one the jury and the witness use.
    L: { on: 'chair arm cap',  armrest: true },
    // heldPen: the grip pose curls the fingers around something, so there had
    // better BE something. Rendered without it first and the hand read as
    // clawing the tabletop -- the pose is only legible with the prop in it.
    // The pen is parented to the hand bone, so it is carried by the pin for
    // free and can never drift out of the grip.
    R: { on: 'tabletop (pen)', lift: 0,     pose: 'pen_hold', heldPen: 'casual' },
  },
  counsel_b: {
    L: { on: 'legal pad (writing)', lift: 0.008, pose: 'pen_hold', heldPen: 'write' },
    R: { on: 'laptop keyboard', lift: 0, pose: 'type_rest', typing: true,
      // The laptop's own deck, from _buildCounselTableProps' constants. The
      // hand is driven here instead of to wherever the arm reaches, and the
      // support height is the deck, not the tabletop.
      // Solved against her own measured arm span -- see the block above the
      // laptop in _buildCounselTableProps for the sweep. This is the wrist;
      // the fingers carry on from here onto the keys.
      // `onSurface` gates this to the venue the laptop is actually IN. Without
      // it the deposition rooms -- which have no counsel laptop and seat
      // counsel_b at (-1.6, -1.0) -- would be asked to send her hand to a
      // courtroom coordinate 4.3 m away. The reach guard would refuse and say
      // so, which is a warning on every deposition load about a thing that was
      // never going to happen. Named rather than left to the guard.
      onSurface: 'CounselTableBTop',
      at: { x: 2.2179, z: 1.1185, y: 0.750 + 0.018 + 0.001 } },
  },
};
// The reporter station's x. See the block above for the solve and
// _buildCourtroomRoom() for the station itself.
// =========================================================================
// COURTSIM-ROOM-002 measured it, COURTSIM-WEB-007 hoisted it and applied it
// everywhere. HOW FAR A WRIST BONE SITS ABOVE A SURFACE ITS PALM RESTS ON.
//
// NOT hand thickness, which is what the surface pass's old 0.055 called
// itself. With a hand solved palm-down at the shared 20 deg palm pitch, the
// LOWEST VERTEX of the hand mesh sits below the wrist BONE by, measured on
// all 22 hands of the eleven seated avatars after the solve:
//     0.0814 .. 0.0856 m,  mean 0.0836
// -- a 4 mm spread across the whole cast, because this distance is set by
// the fixed palm pitch and not by how big anyone's hand is. 0.086 is that
// maximum rounded up, so no hand on this cast can penetrate; the shallowest
// floats 0.0046 m.
//
// It lives HERE, at module scope, because it was a function-local const read
// by only one of the two passes that need it -- and the pass that did not
// read it carried 0.055 instead, which is the founder's "their hands are way
// in there in their tables", to the millimetre.
// =========================================================================
const REST_WRIST_LIFT = 0.086;
// COURTSIM-WEB-007 -- the sound block, on the GAVEL HAND's side of the judge.
// It was at +0.55, which is the judge's LEFT; the gavel is at x -0.20. See
// _buildSoundBlock() for the measurement.
// -0.20 / -3.95 (which is world z -3.00 once BENCH_Z_SHIFT is added), i.e.
// directly under where the gavel head already hangs. MEASURED: the gavel
// head rests at (-0.197, 1.365, -2.998). A gavel lives ON its block, so
// putting the block anywhere else means the strike has to reach for it, and
// this judge cannot -- his shoulder is 0.547 m from the gavel head and the
// old block position was 0.775 m away. Under the gavel, the strike is a
// raise and a drop, which is also what a gavel strike actually is.
const SOUND_BLOCK_X = -0.20;
const SOUND_BLOCK_Z = -3.95;
// COURTSIM-WEB-007 -- the gavel hand's wrist height above the worktop,
// 0.09 -> 0.14. MEASURED on the live build at 0.09: the gavel head's lowest
// vertex sat at y 1.327 against a worktop top of 1.340 -- 13 mm INSIDE the
// bench it is supposed to be resting on, which is visible in the founder's
// own 7:26 screenshot of the bench. 0.14 lifts the head centre to 1.4145,
// which is exactly the sound block's top face (1.381) plus the head's own
// radius (0.0335): the gavel now RESTS ON ITS BLOCK, and judgeInterject()'s
// strike is a return to this pose rather than a reach for a target.
// =========================================================================
// COURTSIM-SURFACES-014 -- 0.14 -> 0.172, AND WHY THE OLD NUMBER WAS BOTH
// RIGHT ON PAPER AND WRONG ON SCREEN.
//
// FOUNDER: "the gavel is going inside of the table little circle thing as
// well. I think it's the same situation as the red carpet, where it's
// registering the table itself as the floor."
//
// HE IS RIGHT ABOUT THE SHAPE OF THE BUG AND I CAN PUT A NUMBER ON IT. This
// constant is a HAND-TUNED OFFSET FROM THE WORKTOP, not a measurement against
// the thing the gavel actually rests on. COURTSIM-WEB-007 solved it once, at
// one instant, from a clean pose, and recorded a 0.0018 m gap. MEASURED here
// across a FULL idle cycle -- 140 samples over 14 s, judge seated, nothing
// else running -- the gavel head's lowest vertex against the sound block's
// top face:
//     min -0.0106   p05 -0.0067   median +0.0140   p95 +0.0310   max +0.0333
// It goes 10.6 mm INSIDE the block once per breath. A single frame could
// never have shown that, and the single frame is exactly what was checked.
//
// THE CONSTANT IS NO LONGER THE GUARANTEE. _clampGavelToBlock() runs every
// frame and lifts the gavel if its head would ever enter the block, so any
// future change to the arm, the breath or the block cannot re-open this. The
// constant's only job now is to put the MIDDLE of the breath on the block, so
// the gavel reads as resting rather than hovering, and to keep the clamp's
// correction small enough to be invisible.
//
// TUNED AGAINST A MEASURED CYCLE, NOT DERIVED ON PAPER. At 0.172 -- 0.140 plus
// the head's own 0.0165 plus a margin -- the same 14 s sample measured
//     min +0.0140  median +0.0306  max +0.0491, clamp never fired
// which never sinks but hovers 3 cm, and a gavel hovering 3 cm over its block
// is the next screenshot. Lowering by 0.0276 puts the median on the block:
//     0.172 - 0.0276 = 0.1444
// and the low half of the breath is then caught by the clamp, whose largest
// correction is a measured 13-14 mm. See the AFTER numbers in the report.
const GAVEL_WRIST_ABOVE_WORKTOP = 0.1444;
// COURTSIM-GAVEL-050 -- the constant above is now only a SEED. The wrist
// height is solved against the block by _landGavelOnBlock(), which measures
// where the head actually lands and moves the wrist by the error; a hand-tuned
// height cannot be right once the handle's direction in the hand changes, and
// this job changed it. Kept, and kept named, because it is still the starting
// point the solve is seeded from and because the seated arm chain reads it.
//
// How far the shaft's AXIS sits off the palm, in metres. The fist tunnel is
// fitted from the finger BONES (see _fitGavelToFist), and a bone is not a
// surface: without this the axis lands inside the palm mesh. Swept live on the
// shipped judge, nearest palm vertex to the axis against a 12.5 mm shaft:
//     0 mm off  ->  1.7 mm   (the shaft is buried in the hand)
//     8 mm off  ->  2.5 mm
//    16 mm off  -> 12.8 mm   <- the shaft is just clear of the palm
//    22 mm off  ->  9.4 mm   (past the hollow; the palm curls back onto it)
// 0.016 is the only unmeasured number left in the grip, and it is one number.
const GAVEL_SHAFT_OFF_PALM = 0.016;
const REPORTER_X = -3.20;
// The reporter's desk top. 0.62 was the only non-0.75 work surface in the
// building; see _buildCourtroomRoom() for why it is now 0.75 like every
// other desk, and _buildReporterLaptop() for what it has to carry.
const REPORTER_DESK_TOP_Y = 0.75;
// DEFECT 1 -- the judge's riser was 1.0 m tall with the judge's own root
// buried inside it. Lowered to 0.49 m for one specific, checkable reason:
// 0.49 (riser) + 0.45 (chair) + 0.06 (lift) = 1.00 m pelvis, which is the
// EXACT pelvis height the judge already had before this pass. The judge's
// head therefore does not move, and camera_controller.js's 'bench' preset
// target (0, 1.5, -3.5) and its measured SIX_SEAT_GUARANTEE stay valid --
// that file belongs to another lane this pass and must not be invalidated.
// 0.49 m is also a real judicial-riser height (two steps).
const BENCH_PLATFORM_H = 0.49;
// The bench desk's top FACE. Unchanged in value from before this pass
// (the old desk was h=0.9 centred at 1.35, top = 1.8) but promoted from a
// literal repeated in two places to one named constant, because the gavel
// fallback placement and the new sound block both have to land on it.
// COURTSIM-FIX-I, Fix 4 -- LOWERED 1.80 -> 1.34, AND THE BENCH MOVED IN
// FRONT OF THE JUDGE. The two are one fix and could never have been done
// separately; COURTSIM-FIX-G-WEB-ROUND2 documented both halves at
// _buildCourtroomRoom() and correctly refused to guess at them.
//
// THE FOUNDER'S REPORT: "the court should be behind his desk... but now he's
// just sitting in front of it. That is a little goofy."
//
// WHY IT COULD NOT BE FIXED BY MOVING THE DESK ALONE. Seated, the judge's
// pelvis is at BENCH_PLATFORM_H + CHAIR_SEAT_H + SEAT_PELVIS_LIFT =
// 0.49 + 0.45 + 0.06 = 1.000. His head bone sits 0.6919 above that (MEASURED
// off the shipped judge.glb: Bip01 Head is +69.187 rig-cm from Bip01, and
// Bip01 carries a 0.01 scale), i.e. 1.6919, and his crown about 1.802.
// A worktop at 1.800 clears his crown by 0.002 m. Put that in front of him
// and he disappears entirely.
//
// THE NEW HEIGHT, DERIVED. The requirement is a real one: a judge is visible
// from mid-chest up above his own bench. His own landmarks, seated:
//     seat pan   0.940      pelvis 1.000      shoulder 1.564      crown 1.802
// (shoulder = pelvis + 0.5642, the measured Bip01 L Clavicle rise). Mid-chest
// is 60% of the way from pelvis to shoulder = 1.000 + 0.6 * 0.564 = 1.338.
// 1.34 is that number. It puts the worktop 0.400 m above his own seat pan --
// high for a writing surface but correct for a judicial bench, where the
// worktop is deliberately raised relative to the seat.
//
// WHAT IT YIELDS, which is what the founder will actually look at:
//     crown above worktop      1.802 - 1.34 = 0.462 m
//     shoulder above worktop   1.564 - 1.34 = 0.224 m
// i.e. head, shoulders and the top of the chest clear the bench. Shoulder to
// crown is only 0.238 m on this rig, so 0.224 m of clearance at the shoulder
// means he reads as sitting AT his bench, not peering over it.
//
// EVERY CAMERA PRESET STILL SEES HIM. Checked as line-of-sight arithmetic
// against the worktop's new centre plane z = -2.97, not by eye:
//     bench    (0,1.7,-1.0)->(0,1.5,-3.5)   crosses at y=1.558, +0.218 clear
//     reporter (0,2.3,7.5)->(0,1.4,-2.6)    crosses at y=1.795, +0.455 clear
//     wide     (0,6.5,10.0)->(0,0.6,-1.5)   crosses well above the bench
// and BENCH_GUARANTEE (Ry 0.80 * 1.05 = 0.84 about the target y = 1.5) frames
// y 0.66..2.34 at the fit plane, which contains both the new worktop top
// (1.34) and the judge's crown (1.802). No preset and no guarantee constant
// needed to change, so VIEWPORT_CONTAIN_001's six-seat proof still holds.
const BENCH_DESK_TOP_Y = 1.34;
// The whole bench carcass translates +0.95 in Z, from a body centred at
// z = -3.95 (BEHIND the judge at z = -3.5) to z = -3.00 (in front of him,
// facing the gallery at +Z). 0.95 is not a round number picked by feel; it is
// the smallest shift that clears his feet. Seated with the shin vertical --
// which is what the measured leg solve produces -- his knee lands one thigh
// forward of his hip, and his toe one toe-length forward of that:
//     knee z = -3.5 + 0.3990 = -3.101      toe z = -3.101 + 0.1381 = -2.963
// (thigh and toe-forward both measured off judge.glb). At +0.95 the bench's
// front panel spans z -2.67..-2.61, leaving 0.293 m of clear foot room in
// front of his toes. The panel is the only full-height element on that face;
// the space behind it is the knee space, as on a real bench.
const BENCH_Z_SHIFT = 0.95;
// DEFECT 1 -- lowered 0.6 -> 0.28 for exactly the same reason, and by the
// same method, as BENCH_PLATFORM_H above. Arithmetic, not taste:
// camera_controller.js's 'witness' preset targets (1.6, 1.3, -1.2) with a
// CLOSE_MARGIN half-extent of 0.20 m, so the witness's head has to stay
// within about 0.21 m of y = 1.3 or the close-up frames his chest.
// Before this pass the witness's pelvis sat directly on the 0.6 m platform
// (head ~1.135, 0.165 below target -- inside). Putting a real 0.45 m chair
// on a 0.6 m platform would have lifted the head to 1.645, which is 0.345
// above target and OUT OF FRAME. 0.28 + 0.45 + 0.06 + 0.535 = 1.325, i.e.
// 0.025 from target -- better centred than before, with a real chair under
// him. Verified against every preset in that file, not just this one.
const WITNESS_PLATFORM_H = 0.28;
// The witness enclosure's own top, in world Y. Capped by a sightline, not by
// taste: the ray from the default reporter camera (0, 2.3, 7.5) to the
// seated witness head (1.6, 1.325, -1.2) crosses the rail plane at z = -1.0
// at y = 1.347. A rail at 1.13 leaves 0.217 m of head clearance; the witness
// preset camera leaves 0.226. Anything approaching 1.35 decapitates him.
const WITNESS_RAIL_TOP = 1.13;
// DEFECT 3 -- two real tiers, per empty-jury-box-in-courtroom.webp, where
// the back row clearly stands a short step above the front. Riser read off
// the photo as roughly a quarter of a seated figure's shoulder height;
// 0.25 m is that step, stated as a read-from-photo estimate, not a spec.
const JURY_TIER_FRONT_H = 0.25;
const JURY_TIER_BACK_H = 0.50;

// Seat keys match _seat_key_for_speaker()'s output on the desktop build
// exactly (godot/courtsim_probe/main.gd) so both builds highlight the same
// speaker for the same transcript line. 'role' names the GLB in
// web/assets/avatars/<role>.glb -- the exact same cast choice as
// courtroom_scene_builder.gd's _place_cast() (bailiff seated at the
// OTHER SPEAKERS station, matching the desktop build's own placement).
//
// pos[1] = the surface the feet stand on. seatY = the surface the seat pan
// is on. See the DEFECT 1/2 block above.
const SEATS = {
  'THE CLERK':       { pos: [-5.5, FLOOR_Y, -2], seatY: FLOOR_Y + CHAIR_SEAT_H, role: 'clerk', label: 'THE CLERK' },
  // COURTSIM-WEB-007 -- x comes from COUNSEL_TABLE_X so the figure, the
  // chair, the table, the hand-rest footprint and the nameplate cannot drift
  // apart. Was a literal -3.3.
  // COURTSIM-PROPS-033 -- chairArms added. See _buildCourtroomRoom's own
  // block at the chair() calls for why, and COUNSEL_HAND_PROPS for the hand
  // the founder asked to put on one.
  'Counsel (Q)':     { pos: [-COUNSEL_TABLE_X, FLOOR_Y, COUNSEL_SEAT_Z], seatY: FLOOR_Y + CHAIR_SEAT_H, role: 'counsel_a', label: 'Counsel (Q)', chairArms: true },
  'THE COURT':       { pos: [0, BENCH_PLATFORM_H, -3.5], seatY: BENCH_PLATFORM_H + CHAIR_SEAT_H, role: 'judge', label: 'THE COURT', elevated: true, chairArms: true },
  // =========================================================================
  // COURTSIM-FIX-I, Fix 5 -- z -1.35 -> -1.75. "THE WITNESS, HER LEGS ARE
  // STILL GOING THROUGH THE LITTLE SEAT THINGS."
  //
  // THE DIAGNOSIS IS NOT WHAT THE BRIEF EXPECTED, AND THE NUMBERS SAY SO.
  // The brief offered three candidates: she is not getting the leg fold, her
  // seat height is wrong, or her seat geometry differs. It is none of them.
  // Running the shipped solve by hand on witness.glb (thigh 0.4261, calf
  // 0.4005, ankle 0.0964 above the root, all MEASURED off that GLB):
  //     drop needed = (0.73 + 0.06) - (0.28 + 0.0964) = 0.4136
  //     cos(hip)    = (0.4136 - 0.4005) / 0.4261 = 0.0307
  //     HIP_DEG     = 88.2, reach = exact, residual zero
  // She IS getting the fold, it IS landing her soles on her own platform
  // height, and 88.2 degrees is a correct seated hip. The fold is fine.
  //
  // WHAT IS ACTUALLY WRONG IS WHERE THE FOLD PUTS HER RELATIVE TO THE RAIL
  // SHE WAS GIVEN. A correct seated fold is shin-vertical, so her knee lands
  // one thigh in front of her hip and her toe one toe-length in front of that:
  //     hip z -1.350  ->  knee z -0.924  ->  toe z -0.788
  // WitnessRail spans z -1.115..-0.995. Her knees clear its FRONT face by
  // 0.071 m and her feet end up 0.207 m in front of the enclosure entirely --
  // over open floor, because WitnessPlatform stops at z = -1.00. Her legs are
  // not going through the seat; they are hanging out the front of the box,
  // which from the gallery is indistinguishable and is exactly the photo.
  //
  // THE FIX IS THE SEAT, NOT THE FOLD. She moves back until her TOES clear
  // the rail's BACK face (-1.115), which is the real constraint:
  //     toe <= -1.115 - 0.06 clearance  ->  knee <= -1.3105
  //                                     ->  hip  <= -1.7366
  // -1.75 satisfies it with margin: knee -1.324, toe -1.188, i.e. 0.073 m of
  // clear air between her toes and the back of the rail. Her chair (0.42
  // deep) then spans z -1.96..-1.54, wholly on the platform (z -2.44..-1.00)
  // with 0.48 m to spare behind it. The witness camera preset moves back by
  // the same 0.40 m in camera_controller.js so the framing distance -- and
  // therefore the solved fov -- is bit-for-bit what it was.
  //
  // CORRECTED FACT WHILE HERE: WITNESS_PLATFORM_H's own comment computes this
  // seat's head height as 0.28 + 0.45 + 0.06 + 0.535 = 1.325. The 0.535 is
  // wrong; head-above-pelvis on witness.glb measures 0.5991, so her head is
  // at 1.389, not 1.325. It changes no decision -- WITNESS_GUARANTEE frames
  // y 0.565..2.035 about the target, and the reporter sightline over
  // WITNESS_RAIL_TOP improves from 0.217 m of clearance to 0.333 m at the new
  // z -- but the number in that comment should not be trusted as measured.
  // =========================================================================
  'THE WITNESS':     { pos: [1.6, WITNESS_PLATFORM_H, -1.75], seatY: WITNESS_PLATFORM_H + CHAIR_SEAT_H, role: 'witness', label: 'THE WITNESS', elevated: true, chairArms: true },
  'Counsel (named)': { pos: [COUNSEL_TABLE_X, FLOOR_Y, COUNSEL_SEAT_Z], seatY: FLOOR_Y + CHAIR_SEAT_H, role: 'counsel_b', label: 'Counsel (named)', chairArms: true },
  // DEFECT 10 (recon D-27) -- the bailiff stood at x = 5.5, exactly on the
  // old JuryPlatform's own x-edge (3.7..5.5), so half his body volume was
  // inside that solid box up to y = 0.4; and he faced -Z, i.e. the rear
  // wall, with every camera preset in front of him at +Z. Moved to the
  // clear floor just off the -Z end of the rebuilt jury box (which now
  // spans z -1.75..1.35, so z = -3.0 is clear by 1.25 m) and turned to
  // face +Z, the well and the gallery -- where a real bailiff stands and
  // looks. Moving him INWARD in x (5.5 -> 5.2) can only shrink the extent
  // camera_controller.js's SIX_SEAT_GUARANTEE was solved against, so that
  // guarantee stays satisfied.
  'OTHER SPEAKERS':  { pos: [5.2, FLOOR_Y, -3.0], seatY: FLOOR_Y + CHAIR_SEAT_H, role: 'bailiff', label: 'OTHER SPEAKERS', faceYaw: 0 },
};

// ===========================================================================
// DEFECT 3 / DEFECT 4 -- jury box layout (recon D-03).
//
// What was there: two boxes. A 1.8 x 0.4 x 6.2 slab and one 0.12-thick rail
// panel across the slab's NARROW -Z end -- i.e. not between the jury and
// the well at all. Six avatars sat directly on the slab. No chairs, no side
// rails, no back panel, no tier. "A big brown cube" was a literal
// description of the geometry.
//
// Rebuilt from the reference photographs already on disk
// (SV4/1/courtroom-jury-box.webp, empty-jury-box-in-courtroom.webp,
// seats-at-courtroom-jury-designed-box.webp). What those photos actually
// show, and what is therefore built below:
//   - a SOLID panelled front rail with a flat projecting cap, running the
//     full length of the well-facing side (not the end);
//   - a return end panel at each end, so the box is an enclosure;
//   - a taller back panel behind the rear row;
//   - TWO rows, the back row on a visible riser;
//   - real upholstered chairs with arms, in an upholstery colour that is
//     not the wood colour of the box.
//
// Six jurors in two rows of three at 0.8 m spacing gives a 3.0 m box, which
// is why the old 6.2 m single-row slab is not simply re-dressed: three
// seats spread over 6.2 m does not read as a jury box in any photo here.
// The rear row is offset half a seat in Z from the front row -- a
// deliberate divergence from the photos, which show the rows aligned --
// because every camera in this room sits at x ~ 0 and an aligned rear row
// is completely hidden behind the front row from there.
//
// DEFECT 4 -- facing. rotation.y is no longer the hardcoded -PI/2; each
// juror's yaw is COMPUTED to aim at the well, using this file's own
// atan2(dx, dz) convention (the same one setActiveSpeaker() uses at the
// bottom of this file, where a model's own forward is +Z). That makes the
// facing self-correcting if a seat ever moves, and it is logged at load so
// a field test produces evidence instead of an opinion.
// ===========================================================================
const WELL_FOCUS = [0, -0.5];        // roughly the middle of the well, between bench and bar
// MEASURED COLLISION, caught in review and fixed here: the first version of
// this rebuild put the box's front rail at x 3.60..3.72. CounselTableB is
// 2.0 m wide centred on x = 3.3, so with its 0.03 overhang its top slab
// reaches x = 4.33 -- the table drove 0.86 m THROUGH the new rail and two of
// its legs ended up buried inside the front tier. The old 0.4 m jury slab
// sat below the 0.75 m table top so there was never an intersection; raising
// the box to a real 1.05 m rail turned that clearance into a collision.
// The table cannot move: camera_controller.js's counselB preset is centred
// on x = 3.3 with a horizontal CLOSE_MARGIN of 0.28, so counsel B has at
// most 0.25 m of travel. The box moves instead, +0.85 in X, which leaves
// 0.12 m between the table's outer edge and the rail and still leaves 0.97 m
// between the box's back panel and the right wall panelling at x = 7.695.
// =========================================================================
// COURTSIM-SEATED-047, DEFECT 6 -- THE JURY BOX COMES OFF THE CARPET AND
// GOES BACK AGAINST THE WALL.
//
// FOUNDER, VERBATIM: "the jury box -- I want it pushed back against the wall.
// It's over the red carpet a little bit and it goes into the red carpet. I
// don't think there's a way around that because it's two graphics stacked ...
// So push the whole thing back so it's flush against the wall behind it, and
// then it's all in the non-carpeted floor area. If you have to shrink it a
// little bit that's fine, but I think it'll fit if we just scoot it back."
//
// HE IS RIGHT ABOUT BOTH THE DEFECT AND THE DIAGNOSIS. MEASURED, live, world
// AABBs, before:
//     WellCarpet        x -5.00 .. +5.00   (y 0.005..0.025, z -5.00..3.00)
//     JuryRailCap       x  4.39 ..  4.63
//     JuryRailFront     x  4.45 ..  4.57
//     JuryTierFront     x  4.57 ..  5.60
//     JuryBackCap       x  6.54 ..  6.78
//     RightWallPanelling inner face x 7.70; RightChairRail inner face x 7.65
// So 0.61 m of the box's front -- the rail, its cap, and the first 1.03 m of
// the front tier -- stands on a 20 mm rug, and there is 0.87 m of empty
// floor behind the back panel. It is exactly what he says it is, and the
// two-stacked-graphics reading is exactly right: a rug and a tier are two
// slabs and there is no z-order that makes that edge look intentional.
//
// THE MOVE. The box goes back until its back cap is 30 mm off the chair rail
// (which is what "flush against the wall" means for a box whose cap projects
// past its panel -- the chair rail moulding, not the plaster, is the first
// thing it can touch), and its front clears the carpet with daylight:
//     rows and back panel  +0.84
//     front rail           +0.72
// The rail moves 120 mm LESS than the rows on purpose, and that is the other
// measured defect in this box: the front row's TOES were 49.7-60.0 mm inside
// JuryRailFront. A seated ankle lands under its own hip, and at the shipped
// 0.59 m from seat mark to rail there was not room for a foot. Widening that
// gap is the same fix as the gallery bench's toe recess, done with the lever
// this box already has.
//
// EVERYTHING IN THE BOX DERIVES FROM THESE FOUR CONSTANTS -- jurors, chairs,
// chair arms, nameplates, tiers, rail, panels and end returns -- so they all
// move together and nothing can be left behind. The after-measurement is in
// the report.
// =========================================================================
const JURY_ROW_X_FRONT = 5.94;
const JURY_ROW_X_BACK = 6.94;
// =========================================================================
// COURTSIM-WIRE-025 -- HOW MANY JURORS. THE ONE-LINE CHANGE.
//
// COURTSIM-LAPTOP-022 item 4 recorded the build as six jurors where the
// smoke list expects twelve, and correctly refused to "fix" it silently:
// six is DELIBERATE here, not the corrupt-file fallback, and a real jury is
// twelve. The founder has not ruled, so the shipped value is unchanged and
// twelve is one line away.
//
// CHANGE THIS AND NOTHING ELSE. Everything below -- the seat Z ladder, the
// box's own Z extent, the rail's raised-panel count and the six chairs --
// is derived from it, so the box and the people in it cannot drift apart.
// At 6 every derived number is byte-identical to what shipped (asserted at
// module load below); at 12 the box grows in Z to hold them.
//
// WHAT TWELVE COSTS, MEASURED (reports/COURTSIM_WIRE_025.md):
//   +6 avatar GLB loads, +draw calls, +triangles -- numbers in that report.
//
// WHAT TWELVE COSTS THAT IS NOT PERFORMANCE, AND NEEDS HIS RULING: the web
// build now owns 21 distinct identities (12 cast + the 9 from
// COURTSIM-ASSETS-023). Twelve jurors means 6 speaking + 12 jury + 4
// gallery = 22 FILLED SEATS. So at twelve, exactly one identity must appear
// twice, and two of the six new jurors are a police uniform (m169) and
// sports kit (m301) -- which ASSETS-023 section 2 deliberately kept out of
// the gallery for reading as a second bailiff and as a bug. See JURY_ROLES.
// =========================================================================
const JURY_SIZE = 6;                 // 6 = shipped. 12 = a full jury.
const JURY_ROWS = 2;
const JURY_PER_ROW = JURY_SIZE / JURY_ROWS;
// MEASURED off chair(): the seat pan is 0.42 wide and the arm caps sit at
// +-CHAIR_ARM_DX (0.235) with a 0.05 cap, so the widest part of a jury chair
// reaches +-0.26 of its centre. The jury chairs are yawed ~84 deg toward
// WELL_FOCUS, so that width lies almost entirely along WORLD Z -- which is
// the axis the seat pitch is measured on.
const JURY_SEAT_HALF = 0.26;
// 0.80 is the shipped pitch and is kept exactly. 0.60 is what twelve needs
// to fit a box that can still clear the bar rail; it leaves 0.08 m between
// adjacent arm caps, which is tight and real (a jury box seat is commonly
// 20-22 inches) rather than an overlap.
const JURY_SEAT_PITCH = JURY_SIZE <= 6 ? 0.80 : 0.60;
// The shipped six are centred on -0.20 and the back row is offset by half a
// pitch so no column of heads lines up. Both properties are preserved.
const JURY_SEAT_Z_CENTRE = -0.20;
function jurySeatZ(row, k) {
  return JURY_SEAT_Z_CENTRE
    + (k - (JURY_PER_ROW - 1) / 2) * JURY_SEAT_PITCH
    + (row === 1 ? JURY_SEAT_PITCH / 2 : 0);
}
// The box holds the chairs, not the other way round -- but at the shipped
// six the literals win, so nothing about the room that has been rendered,
// photographed and signed off moves by a millimetre.
const JURY_BOX_Z_MIN = JURY_SIZE <= 6 ? -1.75 : jurySeatZ(0, 0) - JURY_SEAT_HALF - 0.14;
const JURY_BOX_Z_MAX = JURY_SIZE <= 6 ? 1.35 : jurySeatZ(1, JURY_PER_ROW - 1) + JURY_SEAT_HALF + 0.14;
const JURY_RAIL_X = 5.23;            // rail slab centre; 0.12 thick -> outer face 5.17 (COURTSIM-SEATED-047: +0.72)
const JURY_BACK_X = 7.50;            // back panel centre; 0.12 thick -> outer face 7.56 (COURTSIM-SEATED-047: +0.84)
const JURY_RAIL_TOP = 1.05;          // cap height above the room floor -- chest height on a seated front-row juror
const JURY_BACK_TOP = 1.35;
const JURY_UPHOLSTERY = 0x2e2a2c;    // dark leather, per empty-jury-box-in-courtroom.webp

// Never speak, never highlighted -- exactly mirrors courtroom_scene_builder.gd's
// _place_cast() jury placement (no seat_key registered for jurors).
// pos[1] is the tier top their chair legs stand on; seatY is that chair's
// own seat pan. Same convention as SEATS above.
// =========================================================================
// COURTSIM-FIX-V, DEFECT V-6 -- A FEW PEOPLE IN THE GALLERY.
//
// FOUNDER, VERBATIM: "there's benches where people usually are. Don't fill
// them up. It's not a full trial, but, like, sporadically have a few people
// in there so it feels more like a natural room."
//
// FOUR, not twelve, and DETERMINISTIC -- these coordinates are literals, not
// a seeded scatter, so every session and every screenshot he ever sends back
// shows the same four people in the same four places. A random gallery would
// make his photo reviews unrepeatable, which is the one thing this project
// cannot afford.
//
// GEOMETRY, read off the bench builder below (`GalleryBenchSeat${rowI}`):
//     seat   box [7.5, 0.34, 0.5 ] at [0, 0.33, benchZ]
//     plinth box [7.5, 0.16, 0.38] at [0, 0.08, benchZ + 0.06]
//     benchZ = GALLERY_BENCH_Z0 + rowI * GALLERY_BENCH_PITCH,
//     seat TOP face at y = 0.50, and each bench spans x -3.75 .. +3.75.
// COURTSIM-SEATED-047 split the old single [7.5, 0.5, 0.5] slab into those
// two so the front edge has a 0.12 m toe recess under it. The seat TOP is
// unchanged at 0.50, which is the only number anything else reads.
// The row Z now comes from the two constants declared immediately below, and
// these four spectators are placed FROM them, so a spectator can never again
// be left sitting where a bench used to be (COURTSIM-ROOM-002, item D moved
// every row back by 0.70 m and this list would otherwise have stayed put).
// pos[1] is "the surface their chair legs stand on" and seatY is the pan --
// the same convention SEATS and JURORS use. A gallery bench IS the pan, so
// pos[1] is the floor and seatY is 0.50 directly, rather than
// FLOOR_Y + CHAIR_SEAT_H (0.45). The 0.05 m difference is the bench being
// very slightly taller than the room's chairs, which is true of the model.
//
// PLACEMENT, chosen to read as a sparse public gallery rather than a grid:
// front row two people sitting apart (an aisle-width gap between them),
// second row empty, third row two more offset from the first pair so no
// column of heads lines up. Nobody within 1.2 m of anybody else.
//
// FACING: these are pushed through _loadAvatarSeat with seatKey === null,
// the same path the jurors take, so they get yawToward(WELL_FOCUS) -- every
// spectator faces the front of the room, which is what a gallery does.
//
// COST -- and this is the constraint the brief set, so it is answered with
// numbers, not a reassurance. Four more avatars is +33% on a twelve-avatar
// cast. Per-FRAME they cost essentially nothing measurable: the founder's
// own profiler has `life 0.04 ms` and `idle 0.03 ms` for twelve, i.e.
// ~3.3 us each, so four more is ~13 us on a 16,670 us budget. The real bill
// is LOAD: four more 6-9 MB GLB decodes. That is why they are queued LAST,
// behind the six speaking seats and the six jurors (see _loadVenue's
// concurrency queue), and why the load is concurrency-limited at all --
// nothing in the proceeding waits on a spectator.
//
// ROLES reuse GLBs already being fetched for the cast, so the browser and
// GPU serve them from cache: no new asset is added to the build. Four
// DIFFERENT roles, so the gallery is not four identical clones.
// =========================================================================
// COURTSIM-FIX-AA -- `spectator: true` is a TAG, not a behaviour. It changes
// nothing about how these are built, posed or animated (they still go down
// _loadAvatarSeat's seatKey === null path, identically to the jurors). It
// exists so _loadAvatarSeat can COUNT the ones that actually arrive -- see
// DEFECT AA-5. Placement is unchanged from COURTSIM-FIX-V and is a literal
// table, so it is reproducible by construction: there is no seed to match
// because there is no randomness to seed.
// =========================================================================
// COURTSIM-ROOM-002, item D -- THE WHOLE BENCH BLOCK MOVES BACK 0.70 m.
//
// FOUNDER, VERBATIM (this supersedes his earlier "fit the back door into the
// opening" instruction): "push all of the benches back more, and we should
// take out the back door. That way ... people have to walk in between the
// front bench and the wall in order to get through that center space from
// the two side doors ... we don't want it so their feet or legs are so close
// to the wall ... there'll be less space between the back bench and the
// wall, but that ... looks better there in the back of the room."
//
// 0.70 IS DERIVED, NOT CHOSEN. The binding constraint at the back is the
// rearmost bench's own back panel against the gallery wall's panelling face:
//     GalleryBenchBack2 = box [7.5, 0.55, 0.08] at z = benchZ2 + 0.28
//         -> its rear face is at benchZ2 + 0.32
//     GalleryWallPanelling front face = 7.695   (box [15.8, wh, 0.06] @ 7.725)
// Every other joint in this room is built with a 0.005-0.06 m standoff so no
// two same-normal faces ever share a plane; the back of a bench against a
// wall gets 0.075, the largest of that family, because it is the one gap a
// camera can get inside. So:
//     benchZ2 + 0.32 = 7.695 - 0.075   ->  benchZ2 = 7.30
//     benchZ0 = 7.30 - 2 * 1.10        ->  benchZ0 = 5.10
// i.e. +0.70 on every row. Rows were 4.40 / 5.50 / 6.60, are now
// 5.10 / 6.20 / 7.30. See reports/COURTSIM_ROOM_002.md for the measured
// before/after clearances at both ends of the block.
// =========================================================================
const GALLERY_BENCH_Z0 = 5.10;      // front row centre; back panel of row 2 lands 0.075 clear of the wall panelling
const GALLERY_BENCH_PITCH = 1.10;   // unchanged -- the row-to-row pitch was never the problem
const GALLERY_BENCH_Z = [0, 1, 2].map((i) => GALLERY_BENCH_Z0 + i * GALLERY_BENCH_PITCH);
// The bar rail that divides the well from the gallery (COURTSIM-ROOM-002,
// item C). Declared here and not at the builder because the spectator
// placement, the bench placement and the aisle width are one relationship.
const BAR_RAIL_Z = 3.60;            // slab centre; 0.12 thick -> gallery face 3.66, well face 3.54
const BAR_RAIL_TOP = JURY_RAIL_TOP; // 1.05 -- the bar and the jury box are the SAME height on purpose
const BAR_GATE_HALF = 0.64;         // slab ends; posts at +-0.60 give a 1.06 m clear gate, as the desktop build does
// The door opening, declared once because _buildCourtroomRoom has to break the
// chair rail around exactly the casing _buildDoorSet draws (COURTSIM-ROOM-002,
// item E). Casing outer edge = DOOR_OPEN_W/2 + DOOR_CASE_W + 0.07 = 1.12.
const DOOR_OPEN_W = 1.80;
const DOOR_OPEN_H = 2.70;
const DOOR_CASE_W = 0.15;
// Backband outer face: OPEN_W/2 + QUIRK_BB(0.006) + (CASE_W + 0.07). The
// cornice is wider still (+0.12 each side) but it lives above the chair rail
// on every venue, so the rail break is set by the backband.
const DOOR_CASE_HALF_W = DOOR_OPEN_W / 2 + 0.006 + DOOR_CASE_W + 0.07;   // 1.126
const DOOR_CORNICE_TOP = DOOR_OPEN_H + DOOR_CASE_W + 0.18;        // 3.03
