// =========================================================================
// COURTSIM-SCENE-019 -- DEFECT 2. THE CAPTION WAS AN INTERNAL IDENTIFIER.
//
// The nameplate over each figure is drawn from `def.label`, and for three
// seats that label IS the seat key this file routes on -- 'Counsel (Q)',
// 'Counsel (named)', 'OTHER SPEAKERS'. Confirmed by rendering the live build
// at the founder's own framing this pass: the two counsel plates read
// "Counsel (Q)" and "Counsel (named)" in the frame, which is also what his
// own 11:23 screenshot shows. A seat key is a routing token; it is not a
// thing a courtroom puts on a desk and it should never reach his eyes.
//
// TWO LAYERS, because the name of the person is not always knowable and the
// caption must be right in both cases:
//
//   * THE PLATE DEFAULT, below. What the chair says when nobody has told this
//     file who is sitting in it. Founder-facing vocabulary only -- and for
//     the two counsel chairs it is deliberately the SAME wording the camera
//     preset buttons he already uses say ("Counsel Table A" / "Counsel Table
//     B"), so the plate and the button agree about which side of the room he
//     is looking at. 'OTHER SPEAKERS' becomes 'THE BAILIFF', which is what
//     that seat's avatar (bailiff.glb, m0xx) actually is.
//   * THE PERSON, through setSeatDisplayName() / setProceedingCast() /
//     setActiveSpeaker(seatKey, displayName). Once a cast is known the plate
//     reads 'MS. OKONKWO', which is what the founder asked for.
//
// THE COURT / THE WITNESS / THE CLERK are untouched: those are the real
// captions a real courtroom uses for those seats and they are already right.
// =========================================================================
const SEAT_PLATE_DEFAULT = {
  'Counsel (Q)': 'COUNSEL TABLE A',
  'Counsel (named)': 'COUNSEL TABLE B',
  'OTHER SPEAKERS': 'THE BAILIFF',
};
function seatPlateDefault(seatKey, def) {
  return SEAT_PLATE_DEFAULT[seatKey] || (def && def.label) || seatKey;
}

